  "notes": "RadLab reports absorbed_dose_rate in µGy/hour. Readings are unevenly spaced (about every 20 s inside the South Atlantic Anomaly, about every 100 s elsewhere), so each reading was weighted by the time until the next reading (gaps over 10 min skipped) to get a time-averaged µGy/hour for each UTC day, then multiplied by 24 to get µGy/day. Days with under 20 h of coverage are left out. 27052 readings used. For comparison, a plain unweighted average of all readings would be 533 µGy/day, which over-counts the SAA. This is archived data (RadLab's ISS series ended in late 2024), not a live reading. Gray (Gy) is absorbed energy; sievert (Sv) is the biological dose equivalent, so do not compare these numbers directly with mSv figures."
